<svelte:head><title>Privacy and participation — Voynich@home</title></svelte:head>
<div class="page narrow prose"><p class="eyebrow">Your participation, your choice</p><h1>Keep control of your contribution.</h1><p class="lede">Computation starts after your click. Accounts and public recognition are optional.</p><h2>Guest participation</h2><p>A secure browser cookie proves control of a guest session for up to 90 days. The coordinator stores a hash of the proof, not the cookie itself. Work checkpoints and unsent results use local browser storage. Clearing that storage removes your ability to resume that local work.</p><h2>Accounts and public profiles</h2><p>If you sign in, the authentication service stores your provider account, name, email, sessions, and encrypted provider tokens when returned. Session records can include your IP address and browser information. These details are not published in contribution rankings.</p><p>Your display name, checked credit, and team membership become public only after you choose public visibility. Team names are public. Guest contributions are attached to an account only from a browser that proves control of that guest session.</p><h2>Deletion and research records</h2><p>Account deletion removes the account, sign-in sessions, and public profile. It detaches contribution records and revokes the attached guest proof tokens. Research records remain without your account identity, so published experiments can still be reproduced.</p><h2>Operating data</h2><p>The hosting provider processes network requests and operational logs. The application uses daily IP digests and guest identifiers for short-lived abuse limits. The operator can inspect work failures and moderation records. We do not use advertising trackers or sell contribution profiles.</p><p>Backups can contain earlier account records until their retention period ends. Restoring a backup must reapply the deletion record before public access resumes. The operating guide records the deployed retention and restoration procedure.</p><h2>Software trust</h2><p>Approved worker modules have published digests and restricted imports. Those checks help catch mismatched code; a compromised website could still replace the checking code itself. The native command-line worker provides a separate reproduction path from a verified release.</p><p><a href="/account">Manage sessions, visibility, or account deletion →</a></p></div>
